T2  - Proceedings of the 24th ACM Conference on Economics and Computation
AB  - We study the model of metric voting initially proposed by Feldman et al. [2020]. In this model, experts and candidates are located in a metric space, and each candidate possesses a quality that is independent of her location. An expert evaluates each candidate as the candidate's quality less the distance between the candidate and the expert in the metric space. The expert votes for her favorite candidate. Naturally, the expert prefers candidates that are "similar" to herself, i.e., close to her location in the metric space, thus creating bias in the vote. The goal is to select a voting rule and a committee of experts to mitigate the bias. More specifically, given m candidates, what is the minimum number of experts needed to ensure that the voting rule selects a candidate whose quality is at most ε worse than the best one?
