TI  - Competitive Equilibrium for Chores: from Dual Eisenberg-Gale to a Fast, Greedy, LP-based Algorithm
AU  - Chaudhury, Bhaskar Ray
AU  - Kroer, Christian
AU  - Mehta, Ruta
AU  - Nan, Tianlong
PY  - 2024
T2  - Proceedings of the 25th ACM Conference on Economics and Computation
AB  - We study the computation of competitive equilibrium for Fisher markets with n agents and m divisible chores. Prior work showed that competitive equilibria correspond to the nonzero KKT points of the Nash welfare minimization program, which is a non-convex analogue of the Eisenberg-Gale convex program. We introduce an analogue of the Eisenberg-Gale dual for chores: we show that all KKT points of this dual correspond to competitive equilibria, and while it is not a dual of the non-convex primal program in a formal sense, the objectives touch at all KKT points. Similar to the primal program, the dual has problems from an optimization perspective: there are many feasible directions where the objective tends to positive infinity, and these attract iterative optimization methods. We then derive a new constraint for the dual, which restricts optimization to a hyperplane that avoids all these directions. We show that restriction to this hyperplane retains all KKT points, and surprisingly, does not introduce any new ones. This allows, for the first time ever, application of iterative optimization methods over a convex region for computing competitive equilibria for chores.
